window.docContents[239].resolve({"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Short-exact-sequence/#Chapter7___Definition7___8___2":{"contents":"Definition 7.8.2. A short exact sequence is an exact sequence of the form\n\n0 \\to X \\to Y \\to Z \\to 0.\n\nClearly, 0 \\to X \\to Y \\to Z \\to 0 is a short exact sequence if and only if X \\to Y is injective, Y \\to Z is surjective, and the induced map Y/X \\to Z is an isomorphism. In other words, short exact sequences correspond to extensions of Z by X.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.8. Complexes and cohomology\tShort exact sequence","header":"Short exact sequence","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Short-exact-sequence/#Chapter7___Definition7___8___2"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___3___-Morphisms-of-functors/Natural-transformation/#Chapter7___Definition7___3___1":{"contents":"Definition 7.3.1. Let \\mathcal{C}, \\mathcal{D} be categories and let F, G : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D} be functors between them. A morphism a : F \\to G (also called a natural transformation or a functorial morphism) is a collection of morphisms a_X : F(X) \\to G(X) labeled by the objects X of \\mathcal{C}, which is functorial in X; i.e., for any morphism f : X \\to Y (for X, Y \\in \\mathcal{C}) one has a_Y \\circ F(f) = G(f) \\circ a_X.\n\nA morphism a : F \\to G is an isomorphism if there is another morphism a^{-1} : G \\to F such that a \\circ a^{-1} and a^{-1} \\circ a are the identities. The set of morphisms from F to G is denoted by \\operatorname{Hom}(F, G).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.3. Morphisms of functors\tNatural transformation","header":"Natural transformation","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___3___-Morphisms-of-functors/Natural-transformation/#Chapter7___Definition7___3___1"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/Section-9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents--Section-9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t9. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\t9.1. Lifting of idempotents","header":"Section 9.1: Lifting of idempotents","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/Section-9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents--Section-9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents"}});
